# Fixed-profile CID encoding source variant

This follows the two preserved 600-second full pyparsing preparation failures. The
hoist-only XML profile still spends 41.59 of 106.32 cumulative profiled seconds in
49,019 `validate_cid` calls and 49.85 seconds in 76,022 CID generation calls. Those
times overlap; they must not be added. Generic base32 encoding invokes dynamic type
validation for each character. The trace is `profile_diagnostic_v2_index`.

The second native source variant retains the verified Merkle membership hoist and
changes only fixed-profile CID string encoding. `fixed_cid_encoding_v2.patch`
continues to call native `multihash.digest` on every input. It does not cache source
bytes, digests, canonical payloads, proof results, or read-path success. The existing
source type, structured-value, codec, and read-path decode/recompute guards remain.

For CIDv1/base32/sha2-256 with an exact 32-byte digest, the binary prefixes are
`01 55 12 20` for raw and `01 a9 02 12 20` for dag-json. The latter codec is unsigned
varint 0x129. Canonical strings use multibase `b`, lowercase RFC4648 base32 and no
padding. The implementation checks the native registered codec numbers, profile
constants and multihash envelope, and delegates to the original generic constructor
otherwise. The native hash implementation remains authoritative on every call.

The admitted runtime uses the normal registered base32 codec semantics. Arbitrary
runtime replacement of multibase encoders is outside this closed source profile;
this change is not advertised as a drop-in implementation for arbitrary dynamic
registry mutations. All consumer verification still uses the existing native
decoder and canonicality checks.

`materialize_fastcid_v2.py` constructs the variant through hardlinks and atomically
replaces only the changed `content.py` inode. Original/parent source files remain
unchanged. `runtime_merkle_fastcid_v2_manifest.json` binds the exact source hashes and
parent patch; `run_qualification_v2.py` freezes the actual complete source profile.
The historical and routing services continue using their previous frozen profiles.

Validation before the full run: 2,600 native/generic reference-vector equalities,
50 changed-source rejections, 7 invalid source-type rejections, 5 invalid structured
type rejections, and 3 unsupported-codec rejections. All 64 original content-identity,
Merkle, source-admission, and freshness tests pass. These results are in
`fastcid_validation_v2` and `fastcid_native_regression_v2`.

The first full XML run preserves exactly the baseline state CID and semantic root;
preparation excluding import is 16.731 seconds, versus 64.838 unmodified and 26.296
with the hoist alone. These are one local run per profile. Full pyparsing scanning
is 57.949 seconds with the same 3,957 symbols, 55,859 edges and state CID. Full bundle
completion and any further identity/admission checks must be reported from the
terminal versioned run; the scan result alone is not whole-repository qualification.

No source omission, provider call, repair acceptance, model-token reduction, or
general workload speedup is established by these preparation experiments.
